WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.975
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4425.

2
00:00:00.975 --> 00:00:01.950
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:02.275
<v DOUGLAS MUNGIN>Alright.

4
00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:02.600
Perfect.

5
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:11.050
Alright so today is January twenty-fourth 2015 and I’m Douglas Mungin representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

6
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:17.550
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Sherman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

7
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:24.375
And to kick that off, we are holding Mossville Oral History Day here at Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:26.000
I’m here today with . . .

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:26.667
<v DAREN DOTSON>Daren Dotson.

10
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:29.000
<v MUNGIN>Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

11
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:36.333
The interview should last about fifteen minutes or so, and then at the end we have some . . . A gift bag for you.

12
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:36.667
Alright.

13
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:38.000
So could you please . . .?

14
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:38.333
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Sorry.

15
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.000
One second.

16
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
This is not working right now.

17
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
<v UNKNOWN FEMALE>They want you to say senior?

18
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.000
<v DOTSON>I will say senior this time.

19
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.333
I forgot I got a junior there.

20
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:47.667
<v MUNGIN>[Laughs].

21
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:48.333
Don’t worry.

22
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:54.000
We’re going to give you . . . I’m going to ask you like your full name in a second.

23
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:54.667
You ready?

24
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:55.000
Alright.

25
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
So could you please state your full name?

26
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.511
<v DOTSON>Daren Fitzgerald Dotson, Senior.

27
00:00:59.511 --> 00:00:59.889
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

28
00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:02.156
And where did you grow up?

29
00:01:02.156 --> 00:01:08.578
<v DOTSON>I was . . . I grew up in Maryland, but I spent all my summers here in Lake Charles.

30
00:01:08.578 --> 00:01:11.222
I was born here in Lake Charles.

31
00:01:11.222 --> 00:01:12.733
<v MUNGIN>When were you born?

32
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:13.489
<v DOTSON>In 1965.

33
00:01:13.489 --> 00:01:15.000
November eighteenth, 1965.

34
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:15.500
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

35
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:16.000
Scorpio?

36
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:16.500
<v DOTSON>Yes.

37
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.000
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

38
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
What are your parents’ names?

39
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.219
<v DOTSON>Donald Dotson, Senior and Janie Gardner.

40
00:01:22.219 --> 00:01:24.808
She was Janie Lyons at the time . . .

41
00:01:24.808 --> 00:01:25.178
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

42
00:01:25.178 --> 00:01:26.658
<v DOTSON>Before they got married.

43
00:01:26.658 --> 00:01:28.877
<v MUNGIN>So what did your parents do?

44
00:01:28.877 --> 00:01:30.726
<v DOTSON>My father was a plumber.

45
00:01:30.726 --> 00:01:32.945
My mother, she worked in insurance.

46
00:01:32.945 --> 00:01:35.904
<v MUNGIN>And did they . . . Are they originally from Mossville?

47
00:01:35.904 --> 00:01:37.014
<v DOTSON>My mother is.

48
00:01:37.014 --> 00:01:38.493
She’s originally from Mossville.

49
00:01:38.493 --> 00:01:41.452
She met my father in Moss Bluff [Louisiana]. . .

50
00:01:41.452 --> 00:01:42.562
<v MUNGIN>Moss Bluff, okay.

51
00:01:42.562 --> 00:01:45.151
<v DOTSON>But she did come here to Mossville

52
00:01:45.151 --> 00:01:47.000
<v MUNGIN>So what’s Moss Bluff?

53
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.960
<v DOTSON>Moss Bluff is another city on the outskirts of Lake Charles.

54
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.840
<v MUNGIN>And what are the names of your grandparents?

55
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.000
<v DOTSON>Dave Lyons and Lula Lyons.

56
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.818
<v MUNGIN>Dave and Lula Lyons.

57
00:01:57.818 --> 00:01:58.273
<v DOTSON>[Agrees].

58
00:01:58.273 --> 00:02:02.364
<v MUNGIN>And are they originally from Moss Bluff and Mossville?

59
00:02:02.364 --> 00:02:06.000
<v DOTSON>Well my grandmother, she was originally from Oklahoma.

60
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.636
My grandfather I think he was from here.

61
00:02:09.636 --> 00:02:11.455
It’s hard to say.

62
00:02:11.455 --> 00:02:21.000
I don’t know too much of his background, his history, but I know my grandmother, her history is from Oklahoma.

63
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:21.800
<v MUNGIN>From Oklahoma?

64
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:22.200
<v DOTSON>[Agrees].

65
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.400
<v MUNGIN>So what is your earliest memory of Mossville?

66
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:31.800
<v DOTSON>About maybe two or three years old standing in the front yard of my grandparent’s house.

67
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.200
<v MUNGIN>And where did your grandparents stay?

68
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.800
<v DOTSON>Right on Lyon’s Lane.

69
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.000
They have a street named after them.

70
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.091
<v MUNGIN>How was that?

71
00:02:40.091 --> 00:02:43.364
Growing up on a street named after your family?

72
00:02:43.364 --> 00:02:46.273
<v DOTSON>It’s . . . Gives you something to look forward to.

73
00:02:46.273 --> 00:02:49.182
We got a street named after our grandparents.

74
00:02:49.182 --> 00:02:51.000
It was just that.

75
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:51.620
<v MUNGIN>And so . . .

76
00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.100
<v DOTSON>And it shows how long they been here.

77
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.820
And that the town . . . They thought enough to name that after them.

78
00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.850
<v MUNGIN>So how was it when you were a kid growing up in Mossville?

79
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.090
<v DOTSON>Oh, it was wonderful.

80
00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.500
We had a swimming pool across the street from the house.

81
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.360
It had ten foot diving board.

82
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:14.560
I was terrified of it until I finally . . . Lifeguard chased me off one day and then it was just nothing.

83
00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.490
It wasn’t nothing.

84
00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:16.730
Then . . . It was wonderful.

85
00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:17.660
We had friends.

86
00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:18.280
Horseback riding.

87
00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:18.590
Trails.

88
00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.830
It was . . . just nice.

89
00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:20.760
It was good.

90
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.000
It was good.

91
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.763
<v MUNGIN>So could you describe the property that your grandparents lived on?

92
00:03:25.763 --> 00:03:29.868
<v DOTSON>It’s . . . The house is gone now, but it was a corner lot.

93
00:03:29.868 --> 00:03:31.921
Right here off Old Spanish Trail.

94
00:03:31.921 --> 00:03:36.368
It was right across from the swimming pool and this very rec center.

95
00:03:36.368 --> 00:03:38.763
I used to play in as I

96
00:03:38.763 --> 00:03:39.789
<c.p>was a child.

97
00:03:39.789 --> 00:03:41.158
This was nice, man.

98
00:03:41.158 --> 00:03:47.316
It just . . . Even with the plants down there, you got truck traffic, but it was a community here.

99
00:03:47.316 --> 00:03:48.000
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

100
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:48.905
<v DOTSON>Everybody hung together.

101
00:03:48.905 --> 00:03:51.317
All the families looked out for each other.

102
00:03:51.317 --> 00:03:53.429
<v MUNGIN>How did the house look back then?

103
00:03:53.429 --> 00:03:54.635
<v DOTSON>Oh, it was wonderful.

104
00:03:54.635 --> 00:03:58.254
It was a brick . . . It was cinderblock house, but it was nice.

105
00:03:58.254 --> 00:03:59.460
It was real spacious.

106
00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:03.683
We had a carport, fenced in yard, pecan tree out in the yard, so.

107
00:04:03.683 --> 00:04:05.190
<v MUNGIN>Were you the only grandchild . . .

108
00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:05.794
<v DOTSON>Oh, no.

109
00:04:05.794 --> 00:04:07.000
<v MUNGIN>In the summer?

110
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.550
<v DOTSON>No, it was me and my older brother at first . . . for a while.

111
00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:11.900
Then . . .

112
00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.000
<v MUNGIN>And what’s your brother’s name?

113
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:14.908
<v DOTSON>Donald Dotson, junior.

114
00:04:14.908 --> 00:04:15.514
He’s here.

115
00:04:15.514 --> 00:04:18.239
He’ll be . . . Y’all interview him in a little bit.

116
00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:19.752
It was just wonderful, man.

117
00:04:19.752 --> 00:04:20.358
My grandparents.

118
00:04:20.358 --> 00:04:26.413
Anything we ever wanted they . . . It seemed like when we came here for summer they had it ready for us.

119
00:04:26.413 --> 00:04:26.716
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

120
00:04:26.716 --> 00:04:28.835
<v DOTSON>One year we was . . . This is funny.

121
00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:31.257
We was chasing behind the kids on bicycles.

122
00:04:31.257 --> 00:04:33.982
Next day she went out got us some bicycles.

123
00:04:33.982 --> 00:04:38.220
She knew we wanted to ride horses, she rented a horse for the summer.

124
00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:43.972
We wanted a dog, they had a dog here for the summer for. . It was like heaven to us.

125
00:04:43.972 --> 00:04:45.486
You know what I’m saying?

126
00:04:45.486 --> 00:04:47.000
It was real nice.

127
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.904
<v MUNGIN>And so how was the community back then?

128
00:04:49.904 --> 00:04:50.267
Like . . .

129
00:04:50.267 --> 00:04:52.444
<v DOTSON>I guess they was real friendly.

130
00:04:52.444 --> 00:04:53.533
Everybody knew everybody.

131
00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:57.526
A lot of that was because of my grandparents knew everybody.

132
00:04:57.526 --> 00:05:01.156
They was like some of the first people . . . settled here.

133
00:05:01.156 --> 00:05:02.607
One of the first.

134
00:05:02.607 --> 00:05:04.422
From what I . . . my understanding.

135
00:05:04.422 --> 00:05:05.874
And they knew everybody.

136
00:05:05.874 --> 00:05:12.044
So it was like you had a place to go play and was no cell phones then,

137
00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:16.037
but they had a way of getting in touch with you.

138
00:05:16.037 --> 00:05:17.852
So it was nice, man.

139
00:05:17.852 --> 00:05:18.941
It was nice.

140
00:05:18.941 --> 00:05:26.200
I moved . . . About sixteen I stopped coming down for a while, but every time I came back everybody knew me.

141
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.741
So it was like I never left.

142
00:05:28.741 --> 00:05:36.000
And to the point of when I moved back down here and I came in Mossville, everybody knew me.

143
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.400
<v MUNGIN>When did you move back down to Mossville?

144
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.900
<v DOTSON>Going on five years ago.

145
00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:40.200
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

146
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.200
<v DOTSON>I moved back and I moved into my grandmother house.

147
00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.800
<v MUNGIN>And how has the community changed from when you were a kid?

148
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.000
And then now coming back as like an adult with your own family?

149
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.958
<v DOTSON>Well of course a lot of the families . . . the people you . . . Some of the people you grew up with have passed on,

150
00:05:56.958 --> 00:05:58.312
or they done moved away.

151
00:05:58.312 --> 00:06:01.021
A lot of the business was gone though, compared to . . .

152
00:06:01.021 --> 00:06:04.000
<v MUNGIN>So what businesses were there when you were a kid?

153
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.759
<v DOTSON>When . . . a lot of support . . . Lots of factories.

154
00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:09.172
How would I say . . . Plant support businesses,

155
00:06:09.172 --> 00:06:15.034
which as you see now is coming back, but when I first got here it was gone.

156
00:06:15.034 --> 00:06:17.103
It wasn’t as busy as here.

157
00:06:17.103 --> 00:06:24.345
A lot of the stores . . . like we had . . . When I used to come here it was two convenience stores, they’re gone.

158
00:06:24.345 --> 00:06:25.034
Like that.

159
00:06:25.034 --> 00:06:27.448
But the older people are still here.

160
00:06:27.448 --> 00:06:30.552
The younger generations . . . A lot of them are gone.

161
00:06:30.552 --> 00:06:32.621
<v MUNGIN>Why do you think that is?

162
00:06:32.621 --> 00:06:33.655
<v DOTSON>Probably just work.

163
00:06:33.655 --> 00:06:37.793
When the plants start cutting back it took a lot of work.

164
00:06:37.793 --> 00:06:40.552
People had to go find somewhere to work.

165
00:06:40.552 --> 00:06:42.276
Take care of their families.

166
00:06:42.276 --> 00:06:44.000
Provide for their families.

167
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:44.273
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

168
00:06:44.273 --> 00:06:44.818
And so . . .

169
00:06:44.818 --> 00:06:45.636
<v DOTSON>That’s my opinion.

170
00:06:45.636 --> 00:06:45.909
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

171
00:06:45.909 --> 00:06:47.000
<v DOTSON>That’s my opinion.

172
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.621
<v MUNGIN>And so where did you go to school?

173
00:06:49.621 --> 00:06:51.586
<v DOTSON>I went to school up north.

174
00:06:51.586 --> 00:06:53.224
I went school in Maryland.

175
00:06:53.224 --> 00:06:54.207
Fairmount Heights, Maryland.

176
00:06:54.207 --> 00:06:54.534
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

177
00:06:54.534 --> 00:07:03.052
<v DOTSON>Like I said I spent the majority of my life up there with occasional visits maybe once or twice a year to see my grandmother mainly.

178
00:07:03.052 --> 00:07:06.000
<v MUNGIN>And did you decide to go to college?

179
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.789
<v DOTSON>I went to a community college.

180
00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:08.386
<v MUNGIN>Which one?

181
00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:09.579
<v DOTSON>Prince George’s Community College.

182
00:07:09.579 --> 00:07:12.263
I went there for about a year or two.

183
00:07:12.263 --> 00:07:14.351
Maybe about a semester and a half.

184
00:07:14.351 --> 00:07:18.825
And then I went to Middle Tennessee State for about a semester and a half.

185
00:07:18.825 --> 00:07:20.018
I moved to Nashville.

186
00:07:20.018 --> 00:07:20.912
<v MUNGIN>[Red?] Raiders, right?

187
00:07:20.912 --> 00:07:23.000
<v DOTSON>Not Red Raiders, but Blue Raiders.

188
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:23.467
<v MUNGIN>Blue Raiders.

189
00:07:23.467 --> 00:07:24.167
There we go.

190
00:07:24.167 --> 00:07:27.433
<v DOTSON>But I went to Middle Tennessee State for about a year and a half.

191
00:07:27.433 --> 00:07:29.300
I moved Nashville for thirteen years too, so.

192
00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:29.533
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

193
00:07:29.533 --> 00:07:30.000
<v DOTSON>But . . .

194
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.613
<v MUNGIN>How does that . . . so you went from Maryland to Middle Tennessee, Nashville, now back to Mossville.

195
00:07:36.613 --> 00:07:42.400
Like, how does Mossville compare to those other places that you were growing up?

196
00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.880
I mean that you grew up?

197
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.600
<v DOTSON>Maryland-D.C. area . . . the [DMV?] as we called it . . . Busy.

198
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:49.013
Hectic.

199
00:07:49.013 --> 00:07:50.667
Always something going on.

200
00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:54.387
Nashville . . . I moved to Nashville to go to school.

201
00:07:54.387 --> 00:07:59.347
Watched that city progress and it turned to be a beautiful city.

202
00:07:59.347 --> 00:08:03.067
I see potential in Lake Charles to do that.

203
00:08:03.067 --> 00:08:16.293
So we . . . I can see it coming because I’ve seen it develop in Nashville and how they went from a little so-called country music town to a big city as I say.

204
00:08:16.293 --> 00:08:26.627
And I can see . . . I’ll see Lake Charles and this area is about to do the same with Sasol bringing businesses back into this area.

205
00:08:26.627 --> 00:08:32.000
I’m hoping the best for who stays here . . . people who stays here.

206
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:42.385
<v MUNGIN>And so who were some of the . . . you mentioned earlier how your family . . . Your grandparents were kind of looked up at as upstanding people in the community.

207
00:08:42.385 --> 00:08:47.000
Who were some other community leaders that you remember while growing up?

208
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:51.615
<v DOTSON>It’s . . . Well I can’t say . . . I don’t know by their first names.

209
00:08:51.615 --> 00:08:54.308
I cannot really say their last names.

210
00:08:54.308 --> 00:08:57.000
But the Mosses . . . I remember the Mosses.

211
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:04.308
The Clarks, the Paynes, the Rigmaidens, I got some family out here I can’t think of their last names.

212
00:09:04.308 --> 00:09:07.769
Edward Julia last name . . . Can you go find out?

213
00:09:07.769 --> 00:09:08.538
Edward Julia.

214
00:09:08.538 --> 00:09:10.462
Find out her last name.

215
00:09:10.462 --> 00:09:13.154
I want to say her last name.

216
00:09:13.154 --> 00:09:16.615
And all these people, they like helped raised us.

217
00:09:16.615 --> 00:09:17.769
The names of [. . .?].

218
00:09:17.769 --> 00:09:19.692
They were my grandparents’ friends.

219
00:09:19.692 --> 00:09:20.846
And the kids.

220
00:09:20.846 --> 00:09:25.846
A lot of people I’m naming, we played with their children growing up.

221
00:09:25.846 --> 00:09:33.923
<v MUNGIN>So I keep hearing in our interviews of how like the community helped raise the kids from like door to door.

222
00:09:33.923 --> 00:09:37.000
Did you get that same type of . . .?

223
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:37.304
<v DOTSON>Yes.

224
00:09:37.304 --> 00:09:43.375
Like I . . . it’s . . . if I . . . I could eat next to . . . I didn’t have to worry about if I was hungry.

225
00:09:43.375 --> 00:09:47.018
I could go somewhere and eat because we was all like family.

226
00:09:47.018 --> 00:09:49.446
These people was presented to me as family.

227
00:09:49.446 --> 00:09:52.482
You know how you say . . . everybody says this your cousin,

228
00:09:52.482 --> 00:09:59.768
but when I was brought up and they was telling me this, it was put to me like this was really my family, so.

229
00:09:59.768 --> 00:10:02.196
And that’s the way they treated us, so.

230
00:10:02.196 --> 00:10:06.446
Like I said when we came here to visit it was a good time.

231
00:10:06.446 --> 00:10:09.179
It was like just being around a big family.

232
00:10:09.179 --> 00:10:09.482
<v UNKNOWN MALE>Gordwin.

233
00:10:09.482 --> 00:10:09.786
<v DOTSON>Gordwin?

234
00:10:09.786 --> 00:10:11.000
<v UNKNOWN MALE>Gordwin or Lemelle.

235
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.571
<v DOTSON>Edward Julia Gord . . .

236
00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:14.429
<v UNKNOWN MALE>Gordwin.

237
00:10:14.429 --> 00:10:15.286
<v DOTSON>Gordwin.

238
00:10:15.286 --> 00:10:17.000
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

239
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:22.657
<v DOTSON>And I just want to put they name because she was a big influence in our life, too.

240
00:10:22.657 --> 00:10:22.971
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

241
00:10:22.971 --> 00:10:28.000
<v DOTSON>Like I said we spent time . . . there was houses we can go and spend time with.

242
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.086
Spend time with they children, or you was disciplined like you was family.

243
00:10:32.086 --> 00:10:37.743
What I mean by that is you knew better and they would point you in the right direction.

244
00:10:37.743 --> 00:10:40.571
They didn’t want you going in the wrong direction.

245
00:10:40.571 --> 00:10:45.600
And all these people I never . . . All I can do is got good memories of them.

246
00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:46.543
All these families.

247
00:10:46.543 --> 00:10:47.486
All of Mossville.

248
00:10:47.486 --> 00:10:50.629
I really . . . it was like I said, slice of heaven.

249
00:10:50.629 --> 00:10:51.886
It was a getaway.

250
00:10:51.886 --> 00:10:53.143
We come down here.

251
00:10:53.143 --> 00:11:00.057
You got a swimming pool, you got all the little toys you could possibly want, you got friends, then . . . and the food.

252
00:11:00.057 --> 00:11:01.000
The food.

253
00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:05.495
<v MUNGIN>So where did the young folk hang out, like you were saying like you . . .

254
00:11:05.495 --> 00:11:07.422
<v DOTSON>We used to . . . The swimming pool.

255
00:11:07.422 --> 00:11:09.028
Sorry to cut you off.

256
00:11:09.028 --> 00:11:09.991
The swimming pool.

257
00:11:09.991 --> 00:11:16.092
This rec center we’re interviewing in, the Rigmaiden Rec Center, this was . . . And the ballpark right next to it.

258
00:11:16.092 --> 00:11:18.018
Those . . . This was our play areas.

259
00:11:18.018 --> 00:11:19.945
This is where we played at.

260
00:11:19.945 --> 00:11:25.083
We’d go to a friend’s house, people house, but mainly this is where we played at.

261
00:11:25.083 --> 00:11:27.972
<v MUNGIN>So I got a couple more questions for you.

262
00:11:27.972 --> 00:11:30.220
So what does home mean to you?

263
00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:33.431
So like people keep saying like, “Mossville, that’s my home.

264
00:11:33.431 --> 00:11:36.000
That’s . . .” What does home mean to you?

265
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.250
<v DOTSON>Well I lived a lot of places and I called a lot of places home.

266
00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:49.650
With Mossville, the reason why I say home when I said I’m moving home is because of my grandparents and this where they was.

267
00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:53.850
And this is where a lot of my good memories were spent.

268
00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:55.950
So it just . . . It was home.

269
00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:58.750
It was like . . . It was my grandmother’s house.

270
00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:00.500
It was like coming home.

271
00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:05.400
<v MUNGIN>Did your grandparents like, tell you the history of Mossville or anything like that?

272
00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:11.000
<v DOTSON>Not . . . No, not so much because they were up in the . . . They was up in age.

273
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.100
So basically we got the grandparent.

274
00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:14.850
We didn’t get the history.

275
00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:22.200
As far as I can tell you, no. She just . . . All I ever known was here as far as a child,

276
00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.350
as far as . . . But they never discussed the history.

277
00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:28.500
Basically my mother would kind of talk about it.

278
00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:33.050
But as far as that, that’s as far as I can think of.

279
00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:38.300
As far as I can remember is my grandparents and the people in the neighborhood.

280
00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:39.000
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

281
00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:43.364
<v DOTSON>As far as . . . At that time in my life I wasn’t worried about the history [laughs].

282
00:12:43.364 --> 00:12:43.636
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

283
00:12:43.636 --> 00:12:45.000
<v DOTSON>We worried about fun.

284
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.366
<v MUNGIN>So what does community mean to you?

285
00:12:48.366 --> 00:12:48.847
<v DOTSON>Community.

286
00:12:48.847 --> 00:12:55.580
It was . . . That’s a hard word to describe because people look at that differently.

287
00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:58.466
A good community is family oriented.

288
00:12:58.466 --> 00:13:00.389
Church based kind of.

289
00:13:00.389 --> 00:13:08.084
That’s a hard word to describe because there’s so many ways you can describe a community.

290
00:13:08.084 --> 00:13:10.489
I say close families, man.

291
00:13:10.489 --> 00:13:10.969
Family.

292
00:13:10.969 --> 00:13:12.412
I say family.

293
00:13:12.412 --> 00:13:14.817
<v MUNGIN>Is Mossville still a community?

294
00:13:14.817 --> 00:13:18.183
<v DOTSON>When I was living here, the . . . Yes.

295
00:13:18.183 --> 00:13:24.916
I say so because the people I visited were still the same people, so.

296
00:13:24.916 --> 00:13:27.802
And they genuinely cared about you.

297
00:13:27.802 --> 00:13:33.573
But like I said some . . . A lot of people done moved away,

298
00:13:33.573 --> 00:13:47.038
so my social network of who I used to deal with is not the same now that I got older and a lot of them done left, so.

299
00:13:47.038 --> 00:13:48.000
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

300
00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.343
<v DOTSON>But like I said everybody still remember me.

301
00:13:51.343 --> 00:13:54.687
I was welcomed back here with open arms.

302
00:13:54.687 --> 00:13:59.284
Anything I needed or they could do for me they did.

303
00:13:59.284 --> 00:14:00.955
They tried to help.

304
00:14:00.955 --> 00:14:04.716
They helped me try to get my house together.

305
00:14:04.716 --> 00:14:08.896
So it was . . . Basically it was like I never left.

306
00:14:08.896 --> 00:14:09.313
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

307
00:14:09.313 --> 00:14:10.567
<v DOTSON>To be honest.

308
00:14:10.567 --> 00:14:14.328
<v MUNGIN>So when you moved back where did you work?

309
00:14:14.328 --> 00:14:16.418
<v DOTSON>I worked with my brother.

310
00:14:16.418 --> 00:14:20.597
My father’s part of the family were third generation plumbers.

311
00:14:20.597 --> 00:14:26.030
My grandfather was one of the first master plumbers here in Lake Charles.

312
00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:29.373
My uncles, my father, they all were plumbers.

313
00:14:29.373 --> 00:14:41.075
My brother now, he’s a master plumber and he’s teaching me to be a . . . To get my journeyman and maybe eventually I’ll be a master plumber too, so.

314
00:14:41.075 --> 00:14:44.000
Basically that’s what I do now.

315
00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:48.500
<v MUNGIN>I’ve been noticing like a lot of folks are just like, “My daddy was like this . . . A bricklayer.

316
00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:49.250
I’m a bricklayer.”

317
00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:52.000
Like do you find that a lot in this community?

318
00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:56.193
<v DOTSON>It’s funny because I guess it’s like tradition to some people.

319
00:14:56.193 --> 00:15:01.912
It’s what they do, it’s what they taught, and that’s what makes their living, so.

320
00:15:01.912 --> 00:15:08.011
I know a lot of people like you said, not just here, like in Lake Charles.

321
00:15:08.011 --> 00:15:11.442
Their family was house movers, now they’re house movers.

322
00:15:11.442 --> 00:15:14.492
Their family was demolition people, now they demolition.

323
00:15:14.492 --> 00:15:17.923
I find that a lot here in Lake Charles.

324
00:15:17.923 --> 00:15:21.354
Not just say Mossville, but in general down here.

325
00:15:21.354 --> 00:15:21.735
Yes.

326
00:15:21.735 --> 00:15:26.691
<v MUNGIN>So what’s the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville?

327
00:15:26.691 --> 00:15:27.453
<v DOTSON>It’s funny.

328
00:15:27.453 --> 00:15:33.552
They talk about a lot of the . . . How the plants made people sick and all that,

329
00:15:33.552 --> 00:15:37.746
but these people persevered through all that and they still here.

330
00:15:37.746 --> 00:15:40.796
It was a nice place at the time.

331
00:15:40.796 --> 00:15:44.227
It was a lot of good memories for me.

332
00:15:44.227 --> 00:15:47.276
But as time change, people change, things change.

333
00:15:47.276 --> 00:15:57.188
<c.p>So I’m glad y’all doing this so y’all can have some kind of history and remember because when the town goes and the people get old,

334
00:15:57.188 --> 00:16:01.000
any memory or record of us will be gone.

335
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:01.364
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

336
00:16:01.364 --> 00:16:01.728
<v DOTSON>So.

337
00:16:01.728 --> 00:16:07.916
<v MUNGIN>You’re the first one that we’ve interviewed that actually has mentioned how people started to get sick.

338
00:16:07.916 --> 00:16:11.921
Is that just something that folks just don’t talk about anymore?

339
00:16:11.921 --> 00:16:19.929
<v DOTSON>Well it’s a known fact around here that people of Mossville been fighting about these plants for years, and years, and years.

340
00:16:19.929 --> 00:16:22.841
They bought up the plant to the tracks.

341
00:16:22.841 --> 00:16:28.665
They bought up the houses to the railroad tracks right here to the start of Mossville.

342
00:16:28.665 --> 00:16:35.218
And it’s funny a lot of people was upset because they like, “Well if this place was here,

343
00:16:35.218 --> 00:16:39.222
why . . . I’m pretty sure water travel made . . . got in they water."

344
00:16:39.222 --> 00:16:46.138
It should have passed on, but it’s . . . People got they own reasons behind why they did what they did.

345
00:16:46.138 --> 00:16:51.962
Like I say it’s a known fact a lot of people around here died of cancer.

346
00:16:51.962 --> 00:16:54.146
A lot of people got sick.

347
00:16:54.146 --> 00:17:03.247
But . . . and that these people in this area been fighting with the government and these plants for as long as I can remember about that.

348
00:17:03.247 --> 00:17:05.067
I am not a scientist.

349
00:17:05.067 --> 00:17:08.707
I don’t know, but what goes on speaks for itself.

350
00:17:08.707 --> 00:17:14.531
If you got a lot of people in one area getting sick then there’s something wrong.

351
00:17:14.531 --> 00:17:18.900
A lot of people around here can’t afford . . . Couldn’t afford to leave.

352
00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:28.000
So what Sasol did helped some of the people out, but in general you have to talk to mainly the older generation about that.

353
00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:34.531
<v MUNGIN>They seem to kind of keep it . . . Not quiet, but it’s just something that they don’t want to discuss so much anymore.

354
00:17:34.531 --> 00:17:36.312
<v DOTSON>Well it was . . . It’s public record.

355
00:17:36.312 --> 00:17:42.844
And after a while, for fighting thirty some years about this, this can’t . . . It’s kind of like throwing water in the wind.

356
00:17:42.844 --> 00:17:46.406
It’s going to blow back on you, you know what I’m saying?

357
00:17:46.406 --> 00:17:47.000
<v MUNGIN>[Agrees].

358
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.562
<v DOTSON>So maybe they’re looking at it like that.

359
00:17:49.562 --> 00:17:52.124
Maybe they’re just tired of . . . And then again,

360
00:17:52.124 --> 00:17:57.889
like I say Sasol came here and they started offering these people voluntarily to get . . . Move away, so.

361
00:17:57.889 --> 00:18:02.373
It’s kind of late for some people, but then it’s a blessing for others.

362
00:18:02.373 --> 00:18:02.693
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

363
00:18:02.693 --> 00:18:05.575
So is there anything else you want to add?

364
00:18:05.575 --> 00:18:13.902
We’re about to wrap up the interview, so is there anything else you want to add about Mossville, or about yourself, or just any final words?

365
00:18:13.902 --> 00:18:14.222
<v DOTSON>Mossville.

366
00:18:14.222 --> 00:18:20.307
A lot of the . . . older generation when I was growing up, what I did remember they was all classy.

367
00:18:20.307 --> 00:18:22.229
They always dressed up every day.

368
00:18:22.229 --> 00:18:26.712
That’s just one of the things that made to me I thought was special.

369
00:18:26.712 --> 00:18:32.157
You go to somebody house and knock on the door, the mother, father . . . They was dressed up.

370
00:18:32.157 --> 00:18:35.359
They didn’t just throw on T shirts and come to . . .

371
00:18:35.359 --> 00:18:36.000
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

372
00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.782
<v DOTSON>Like my grandma, she used to tickle me to death.

373
00:18:38.782 --> 00:18:42.120
I came down here . . . I brought my kids down here one time.

374
00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:43.511
We was coming to visit.

375
00:18:43.511 --> 00:18:49.075
And like I said, she was the type of person . . . She’d get up and put her face on and everything.

376
00:18:49.075 --> 00:18:51.857
And then don’t go nowhere, but that’s how they did.

377
00:18:51.857 --> 00:18:53.248
That’s just the older generation.

378
00:18:53.248 --> 00:18:54.917
And she wouldn’t let me in.

379
00:18:54.917 --> 00:18:58.256
She came to the door without all her makeup and stuff on.

380
00:18:58.256 --> 00:18:59.925
It just tickled me to death.

381
00:18:59.925 --> 00:19:02.985
She looked and she looked at me said, “Hold on baby.”

382
00:19:02.985 --> 00:19:04.098
She shut the door.

383
00:19:04.098 --> 00:19:05.767
About five minutes later she came.

384
00:19:05.767 --> 00:19:08.549
She had lipstick, blush on, and her hair done up.

385
00:19:08.549 --> 00:19:13.000
And it just tickled me to death that she didn’t want no one to see . . .

386
00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:14.381
<v MUNGIN>Like, “I’m your grandson!”

387
00:19:14.381 --> 00:19:19.558
<v DOTSON>She didn’t want us to see her . . . But that’s just how they were back then.

388
00:19:19.558 --> 00:19:20.593
Things change now.

389
00:19:20.593 --> 00:19:20.938
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

390
00:19:20.938 --> 00:19:25.080
<v DOTSON>It’s not the same, but that kind of . . . Everybody was like that.

391
00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:30.947
It just wasn’t . . . it’s just . . . it was . . . it’s kind of hard to describe, but it was nice.

392
00:19:30.947 --> 00:19:33.708
People took pride in that kind of stuff.

393
00:19:33.708 --> 00:19:35.434
They took pride in Mossville.

394
00:19:35.434 --> 00:19:43.027
Mossville was . . . the high school was a powerhouse as far as when they did . . . The rec center put out good ball teams.

395
00:19:43.027 --> 00:19:44.752
It was just nice, man.

396
00:19:44.752 --> 00:19:46.133
It was just nice.

397
00:19:46.133 --> 00:19:46.478
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

398
00:19:46.478 --> 00:19:48.894
I think that wraps up the interview.

399
00:19:48.894 --> 00:19:49.239
Alright.

400
00:19:49.239 --> 00:19:49.584
Cool.

401
00:19:49.584 --> 00:19:52.000
Thank you so much for that.

402
00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:52.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4425.

403
00:19:52.429 --> 00:19:52.857
End Session I.]
